\chapter{Operating-system, I/O, and time facades}
\label{ch:58}
\index{Operating-system, I/O, and time facades}

The I/O, OS, Unix, environment, time, and terminal modules expose specific slices of the host. Distinguish a text snapshot from a byte transfer, an environment key that is absent from a key set to an empty string, and a wall-clock timestamp from a monotonic duration. The terminal profile may require a real terminal; a fixture built around redirected standard output exercises a different host path.

\section{Keep failure observable}
Use a tempfile fixture for the success case and a known missing path for the failure case. If a wrapper returns a success flag and data, assert both. Include a binary fixture with zero bytes where the API admits it. Source \OnxCodeTerm{shexp} helpers operate on their supported command representations. Process execution uses the explicit cooperative profile in \cref{ch:45} or an appropriate native interface. A project build's sandbox policy is a separate decision covered in \cref{ch:66}.

\section{Choose the representation before opening the file}
Consider a two-byte file containing zero followed by the letter A. The binary facade represents bytes as pairs of hexadecimal digits, so the text to pass to its write operation is \OnxCodeTerm{"0041"}. A successful binary write produces two file bytes; a successful text write of those same four characters produces four file bytes instead. Round-tripping through the same mistaken interface on both ends could hide the error. Check the file length or use an independent byte reader as well as comparing the high-level value. Conversely, for an ordinary text fixture write a short line, check the Boolean success result, append another line, then use the whole-file text read to compare the concatenation. That read returns text directly, not the Boolean returned by the write.

Another representation trap concerns the command helpers. Construct an exec node, wrap it in a change-directory node, and call \OnxCodeTerm{shexp\_render}. Follow the implementation: it takes the head of the outer list and returns its text. The result is the tag \OnxCodeTerm{chdir}, not a shell script that can be replayed and not proof that the process changed directory. Wrapping two actions in a sequence similarly gives a node headed by \OnxCodeTerm{seq}. If a test needs to verify the directory or arguments, inspect the corresponding children of the persistent tree. Checking only its rendered tag tests the outer constructor and leaves the actual operands unexamined.

